// Release-path override. Applied only by the workflow that publishes:
//
//     npm run tauri build -- --config src-tauri/tauri.release.conf.json5
//
// It carries exactly one key, and it must stay that way.
//
// WHY createUpdaterArtifacts IS HERE AND NEVER IN tauri.conf.json
//
// This key is what the bundler gates updater signing on — tauri-cli 2.11.4
// (crates/tauri-cli/src/interface/rust.rs) enables the updater bundle only when
// `create_updater_artifacts != Updater::Bool(false)`, and the default is
// Bool(false) (tauri-utils 2.9.3, config.rs:1540). The moment it is true, the
// bundler demands TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY. A pull request from a fork cannot
// hold that secret, so this key in the base config would fail CI for every
// outside contributor — build.yml builds installers on every PR. build.yml must
// therefore never pass this file; only the release workflow does.
//
// The public key deliberately does NOT live here. It stays in tauri.conf.json,
// because the updater plugin's config has `pubkey: String` with no serde
// default: without it, plugin initialization fails and the app does not launch
// at all. A pubkey alone demands no secret, so it costs fork builds nothing.
//
// JSON5 rather than JSON only so it can carry these comments — the CLI parses
// JSON, JSON5 and TOML for --config.
{
  bundle: {
    createUpdaterArtifacts: true,
  },
}
